Firstmate fork: PR readiness wake
=================================

Registers the fork-owned source that will wake Firstmate when a pull request it
may merge on its own authority has finished: every required check reported green
at the recorded head. That wake is the capability that closes the gap where a
green pull request waits for the next heartbeat or for the captain to notice it.

Slice T1 shipped the adapter and its registration contract with an empty
readiness seam. This slice (T2) fills that seam with the conservative readiness
predicate described below, so an eligible pull request whose every unwaived
required check has reported green at its recorded head now produces the advisory
wake. The one-shot per-head latch is still owed by a later slice, so a pull
request that keeps looking ready is announced again by every poll:
acknowledging a result settles that one sequence, and the next poll captures a
new one ("Event delivery and acknowledgement").

It is fork-owned. Everything lives under fork/pr-green/, upstream code stays
byte-identical, and it reaches Firstmate through the same documented
extension seam the Discord bridge uses: an external process-event adapter,
registered in the home, that emits one ordinary process-event wake which
Firstmate's existing supervision already consumes.

Like fork/README, this file is plain text with no extension on purpose:
upstream's documentation-audience check requires every tracked *.md, *.mdx,
*.rst, and *.txt file to be registered in an upstream-owned inventory, and the
fork does not edit that inventory.


Advisory by construction
------------------------

The wake this source emits is advisory: it says "look at this pull request",
never "merge it". The constraints below bind the predicate installed at the
seam, and the shipped code keeps them.

  - bin/fm-pr-merge.sh remains the only merge authority. It re-reads every
    required check at merge time and refuses a red pull request, a required
    check that has not reported, an inconsistent head, and a draft.
  - Nothing in this package merges, arms a merge, answers a finding, writes a
    waiver, records approval, or records merge authority. It never rewrites a
    task record.
  - Because the readiness test cannot share core internals, the predicate is an
    independent, advisory implementation. A divergence from the merge gate can
    cost a wasted wake or a missed one; it cannot cause a bad merge.
  - Silence must be the default for the readiness judgment: a missing required
    check, an unreadable or failed check read, a stale head, an unclear
    conclusion, or any doubt must produce no readiness wake, never a claim of
    green. A failure of the adapter's own read is not that case: it is captured
    as host failure evidence and classified apart from readiness evidence (see
    "Event delivery and acknowledgement" below), never as a green pull
    request.

Only work Firstmate may land on its own authority is eligible, so a wake can
only ever be a question Firstmate is already allowed to answer by itself.


Supported scope
---------------

  Providers     GitHub only. gitlab, gerrit, and forgejo records can never be
                offered to this source: a URL that is not a GitHub pull request
                URL is refused before any readiness logic runs, and a GitHub
                Enterprise host is accepted only when this home lists it in
                config/github-hosts, exactly as upstream's own GitHub host
                rule requires.
  Delivery      mode=no-mistakes and mode=direct-PR. A local-only task is
                never eligible, because its landing path is a local merge
                firstmate does not get to decide alone.
  Merge posture yolo=on only. A yolo=off task's pull request is merged only
                after the captain's explicit word, so a readiness wake for it
                would be an invitation firstmate may not act on.
  Kind          Ships only in practice: secondmate records (kind=secondmate or
                mode=secondmate) are refused, because a secondmate is
                reconciled by its own home and is not itself a pull request
                task.
  Armed         state/<id>.meta must carry pr=<github pull URL> and
                pr_head=<sha>, and the armed merge poll's state/<id>.check.sh
                sidecar must still exist. Upstream writes those fields when it
                arms the poll (bin/fm-pr-check.sh), so a pr= left behind by a
                disarmed or finished poll is not eligible.

A record that is not a plain regular file, that carries a duplicate key, that
cannot be read, or whose task id is not path-safe is skipped silently. Each
home reads only its own state directory: a secondmate home that wants this
capability registers its own source against its own home, and no home ever
evaluates another home's records.


Files
-----

  extension/firstmate-extension.json  the process-event-adapter/1 manifest:
                                      id org.firstmate.fork.pr-green, adapter
                                      name "pr-green", consent task-metadata,
                                      credential-store, network.
  extension/fm-pr-green.mjs           the adapter: config_ref validation, the
                                      scope gate above, the bounded long poll,
                                      and the protocol verbs.
  extension/readiness.mjs             the readiness seam. It is the whole of
                                      the adapter's judgment: the readiness
                                      predicate and the advisory body.
  fork/tests/pr-green-fake-gh         the fixture GitHub CLI the fork tests serve
                                      recorded answers from; it never contacts a
                                      forge.
  fork/tests/fm-fork-pr-green.test.sh the adapter tests: the scope gate against a
                                      fixture home, a malformed or unusable
                                      config_ref and the classification of the
                                      failure it captures, the installed
                                      predicate turning a green fixture pull
                                      request into one advisory result through the
                                      real binding, registration, and runner,
                                      silence while a check is still running, and
                                      the wake and acknowledgement path driven
                                      through a package copy whose seam is a
                                      fixture stub.
  fork/tests/fm-fork-pr-green-readiness.test.sh
                                      the predicate's own suite: ready, every
                                      not-ready shape, each unreadable required
                                      source, the supersession rules, the
                                      producer-app binding, the read budget, and
                                      the read cost of each case, all against the
                                      fixture CLI.


The readiness seam
------------------

The whole of the adapter's judgment lives behind one explicit seam, so no other
part of the package can quietly acquire an opinion about a pull request:

  decideWake(entry, ctx) -> null | {advisory: "<wake body>"}

  entry is one task that already passed the scope gate above, with its validated
  pull request identity and its recorded head - the state/<id>.meta pr_head= the
  gate already validated. ctx carries the absolute home, state, and config paths
  and a stderr logger.

A wake is produced only when all of this holds at once:

  open       the live pull request state is OPEN and isDraft is exactly false.
  head       the live head is a valid commit id and equals the recorded head. A
             head the record does not carry is never inferred, and a
             disagreement is silence rather than a guess.
  checks     every reported check is green, judged with the merge gate's own
             semantics: a check run counts only through its current run, so a
             cancelled run beside a later passing re-run of the same name is
             superseded, while a still-running, queued, undated, or tied run
             stays red; unnamed runs are grouped alone; status contexts are never
             grouped or superseded; and COMPLETED SUCCESS, NEUTRAL, and SKIPPED
             are green while everything else is not.
  required   the applicable classic branch protection and active rulesets are
             both read completely, and every required context they name has
             reported at that live head, with a matching producer app for a
             producer-bound requirement that reported as a check run.

A reported SKIPPED or NEUTRAL conclusion is green exactly as the merge gate reads
it, because that gate is the authority that re-reads every requirement. A
required context that never reported at all is the other case: it is not green,
and it keeps the whole read silent.

Silence is the answer to every other outcome: a lookup failure, a timeout, an
incomplete or malformed answer, an unparsable document, a rollup entry outside
the two shapes the rollup carries, an unreadable required-context source, a
partially successful read, or any doubt at all. A
green-looking rollup beside an unreadable required-context source is silence,
never green. The merge gate's narrow "rulesets unavailable on this plan"
exception is deliberately not mirrored: here an unreadable required set is not
readiness, because a missed wake costs a wait while a false green costs trust.

The predicate re-expresses only what an advisory verdict needs, from the
semantics bin/fm-pr-merge.sh documents. It never calls the merge command and
never writes a task record, so a divergence can cost a wasted wake or a missed
one and never a bad merge. extension/readiness.mjs's header is the single owner
of the contract, including the failure directions a later slice still owes: the
per-head latch, and an interrupted latch write that fails toward one possible
duplicate wake rather than a permanently suppressed one.


Read cost and the forge binary
------------------------------

The common not-ready case costs exactly one forge read: the live pull request
view. The required-context reads, and the check-run producer read a
producer-bound requirement needs, happen only after that view already looks
fully green at the recorded head. Every read of one poll shares one bounded
budget of 25 seconds, well inside the binding's 300-second default invoke
timeout, so a slow forge produces silence inside that budget instead of
outliving it however many tasks the poll has to evaluate. The adapter also asks
the seam about one pull request at most once per poll rather than once per
internal rescan, and once one poll has evaluated its eligible tasks it returns
them together as one result.

The extension host gives a bound package a minimal environment, so the gh on the
operator's PATH is usually not resolvable inside it. The predicate therefore
resolves the binary explicitly, and predicts nothing:

  config/pr-green-gh   optional. One absolute path to the gh CLI. When this file
                       exists it is authoritative: an unreadable, multi-line, or
                       non-executable answer is silence rather than a fallback.
  otherwise            the standard install locations are probed in one fixed
                       order (Homebrew's, /usr/local/bin, /usr/bin, /bin, then
                       beside the interpreter). No gh means no wake.

Authentication is gh's own, which is why the binding requires the credential-store
consent: the operator's existing gh login is used and no credential is ever read,
written, or handed to the package. state/pr-green/read-budget-ms (200-25000) may
shorten the read budget for tests or an unusually slow host; it can never
lengthen it.


Registration
------------

Registration is an operator step; this repository change activates nothing.
The package must live outside every Git project, as upstream requires for any
bound package. With FM_HOME exported as the home the source belongs to:

  mkdir -p "$HOME/.local/share/firstmate-packages"
  cp -R fork/pr-green/extension "$HOME/.local/share/firstmate-packages/pr-green"
  bin/fm-extension.sh bind "$HOME/.local/share/firstmate-packages/pr-green" \
    --adapter pr-green --trust-same-user-code \
    --consent task-metadata --consent credential-store --consent network
  bin/fm-procevent.sh register-extension pr-green pr-green \
    --config-ref "home:$FM_HOME"

The consents are honest rather than sandboxing: the package reads task records
under the registered home (task-metadata), uses the operator's existing gh login
(credential-store), and reaches the forge the pull request belongs to (network).
The config reference is the private, non-secret statement of which home the
source belongs to; it appears in envelopes and registrations and never holds a
credential.

`register-extension` prints the owner token and the exact retirement command.
Keep the token. To change the package, withdraw and re-bind:

  bin/fm-procevent.sh retire pr-green --if-owner <owner-token>
  bin/fm-extension.sh retire-binding org.firstmate.fork.pr-green \
    --if-binding-digest <binding-digest>
  (then bind and register the new copy again)

A registered source keeps Firstmate's supervision running and the watcher
restarts it every cycle, so retire the source to stop it.


Event delivery and acknowledgement
----------------------------------

When the seam reports ready pull requests, the adapter returns one result
carrying an advisory block for each of them - never only the pull request whose
task id sorts first. Core captures it as external evidence and publishes the
ordinary bounded process-event wake, so nothing new is introduced into
Firstmate's supervision. The path is the one the fork tests already drive with a
stub seam:

  check: procevent pr-green pr-green <sequence>

  classify    bin/fm-procevent.sh classify <result-file> prints
              pr-ready-advisory for a readiness body and pr-ready-error for a
              host failure record, so a failed read is never presented as
              readiness evidence and readiness evidence is never read as a
              merge or an approval.
  terminal    no. The source never concludes itself, so it keeps listening for
              the next pull request.
  silent      no. An unhandled readiness result is re-announced until firstmate
              acknowledges it, so a single wake is never lost to a missed turn.
              That retry is per result sequence, not per pull request.
  acknowledge bin/fm-procevent.sh handled pr-green <sequence>

The latch that keeps one wake per pull request per head will live in the seam,
not in core, so a re-announced result and a re-polled head are different things:
the first is core's ordinary retry of one sequence until `handled`, the second
is a new sequence the next poll captures, and the seam's own deduplication is
what a later slice adds to stop it. bin/fm-procevent.sh list shows the pending
count.


Latency
-------

The source holds one bounded long poll (up to 50 seconds) and re-scans the
home's task records every 5 seconds inside it, so a newly armed task is picked up
mid-poll rather than at the next cycle. That rescan is cheap and local: the forge
is asked about each eligible pull request at most once per poll, so the rescan
never multiplies network reads. When the poll returns no result the runner exits
and the ordinary supervision cycle restarts it (FM_POLL, 15 seconds by default),
so the worst case for a green pull request is roughly one poll plus one cycle.
Only tests shorten those waits: they set state/pr-green/poll-wait-ms (100-50000)
and state/pr-green/read-budget-ms (200-25000) to keep their runs fast.


Adapter state
-------------

The fork's own adapter state belongs under <home>/state/pr-green/ and is the
seam's to own. This slice still writes nothing there and creates nothing: the
adapter and the predicate are pure readers of state/<id>.meta,
state/<id>.check.sh, config/github-hosts, the optional config/pr-green-gh and
poll-wait/read-budget knobs above, and the forge. A later slice's latch marker
lives in that directory, and that marker is the only thing this capability is
ever meant to write.


Known limits
------------

  - No per-head latch yet: a green pull request keeps producing a fresh result
    and wake on every poll, including after an acknowledgement, which the
    process-event machinery absorbs but does not deduplicate.
  - One result carries at most 30000 bytes of advisory blocks. A home with more
    simultaneously ready pull requests than fit leaves the ones past the bound
    out of that result instead of publishing a result the host would refuse.
  - The predicate is a fork-owned re-expression, not the merge gate's own code.
    It is deliberately the stricter of the two where they can differ (an
    unreadable required-context source is silence here), so the failure it can
    cause is a missed or late wake, never a green claim the merge gate would
    refuse on its own reading.
  - The forge CLI is resolved explicitly (see "Read cost and the forge binary"),
    so an unusual install layout needs config/pr-green-gh. Without a usable gh
    the source is silent.
  - GitHub only, by design: gitlab needs a JSON-based required-check read,
    forgejo needs paginated statuses, and gerrit has no merge path in firstmate
    at all, so gerrit must never wake.
  - A GitHub Enterprise host must be listed in this home's config/github-hosts.
    An unlisted or unreadable list means silence for that host, never a guess.
  - pr_head is recorded only by upstream's GitHub arming path, so a task whose
    record has no head is skipped even when its pull request is green.
  - Two local homes each maintain their own registration, source, and adapter
    state; neither evaluates the other's records.
  - Nothing here has been exercised against a live repository: the tests run
    the real extension host, the real process-event runner, the real readiness
    predicate, and real upstream task records, with a fixture GitHub CLI and no
    network. The 25-second read budget has not been measured against a real
    forge on a loaded machine.
